WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.814 --> 00:00:09.730 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Japan's prefectures, part 3 of 4. First, I clear the chosen names
for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.391 --> 00:00:22.198 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 15 of the Japan regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.078 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Saitama into view.

4
00:00:29.006 --> 00:00:40.477 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Saitama is inland just north of Tokyo on the Kantō plain, with Gunma above it
and the old warehouse town of Kawagoe in its southern half.

5
00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:48.880 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It has no coastline at all and the longest average commute in Japan, because
much of it sleeps here and works in Tokyo.

6
00:00:49.532 --> 00:00:57.253 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the little Edo of Kawagoe’s warehouse streets, on the plain where
people commute into the capital every morning.

7
00:00:58.001 --> 00:01:00.126 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Gifu into view.

8
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:17.343 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Gifu is inland in the centre of Honshū, with Nagano and Toyama above and Aichi
below, and the city of Gifu in the plain beside the Nagara river.

9
00:01:18.001 --> 00:01:32.401 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name was invented in 1567 by Oda Nobunaga, who took one character from the
Chinese mountain where the Zhou dynasty began and one from Confucius’s
birthplace, announcing that he meant to rule the country.

10
00:01:33.058 --> 00:01:42.485 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the steep thatched roofs of Shirakawa-gō that shed metres of snow,
and the cormorant fishing families on the Nagara river.

11
00:01:43.242 --> 00:01:45.460 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tokyo into view.

12
00:01:53.157 --> 00:02:05.615 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tokyo is the capital prefecture in the middle of the Kantō plain, facing the
Pacific and Tokyo Bay, with Saitama to the north and Chiba and Kanagawa to the
south.

13
00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:18.759 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Edo was already the world’s largest city by 1720, with over a million people,
and greater Tokyo today is the largest metropolitan area in the world, about
37 million people.

14
00:02:19.415 --> 00:02:31.060 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the eastern capital, renamed in 1868 when the emperor moved here,
with its fire brigades once organised street by street.

15
00:02:31.732 --> 00:02:34.112 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yamanashi into view.

16
00:02:38.655 --> 00:02:50.637 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yamanashi is inland in the mountains north-west of Tokyo, with the city of
Kōfu in its central basin and Mount Fuji standing on its border with Shizuoka.

17
00:02:51.292 --> 00:02:58.409 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The five lakes at the foot of Fuji are here, and Japanese winemaking began in
this prefecture with the koshū grape.

18
00:02:59.068 --> 00:03:06.533 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the mountain pear and the vineyards of the old koshū grape, with Fuji
on the edge of the basin.

19
00:03:07.212 --> 00:03:09.383 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Chiba into view.

20
00:03:13.441 --> 00:03:24.064 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Chiba wraps around the Bōsō Peninsula on the Pacific east of Tokyo, with the
city of Chiba on the bay and Ibaraki along its northern edge.

21
00:03:24.715 --> 00:03:34.049 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Narita, Japan’s main international airport, is here, and so is Tokyo
Disneyland, which is not in Tokyo.

22
00:03:34.712 --> 00:03:43.663 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the thousand leaves, the peanuts, and the soy sauce that has been
brewed at Noda since 1661.

23
00:03:44.345 --> 00:03:48.049 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kanagawa into view.

24
00:03:50.568 --> 00:04:00.251 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kanagawa faces the Pacific on the coast south-west of Tokyo, with the city of
Yokohama on the bay and Yamanashi and Shizuoka behind it.

25
00:04:00.915 --> 00:04:14.266 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yokohama was a fishing village until 1859, when it was opened to foreign
trade, and Kamakura, seat of the shogunate from 1185 to 1333, lies on its
western shore.

26
00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:22.932 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the fishing village that became a great port, and the bronze Buddha
sitting in the open since a tsunami took its hall.

27
00:04:23.609 --> 00:04:26.291 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kyōto into view.

28
00:04:30.004 --> 00:04:41.602 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kyōto sits in the centre of Honshū, reaching the Sea of Japan in the north,
with Shiga to the north-east and Ōsaka to the west and the old capital city of
Kyōto in its southern half.

29
00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:59.241 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was the capital of Japan for more than a thousand years, from 794 to 1868,
and it was deliberately left off the atomic bomb target list, which is why its
1,600 temples survive.

30
00:04:59.894 --> 00:05:06.245 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the thousand-year capital, and the Kiyomizu temple stage built
without a single nail.

31
00:05:06.924 --> 00:05:08.921 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Shiga into view.

32
00:05:12.926 --> 00:05:23.712 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Shiga is inland in the centre of Honshū and wraps around Lake Biwa, with the
city of Ōtsu on the eastern shore and no sea at all.

33
00:05:24.368 --> 00:05:33.668 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lake Biwa, which fills a sixth of the prefecture, is the largest lake in Japan
and one of the oldest on earth, at about four million years.

34
00:05:34.318 --> 00:05:43.234 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the ancient lake, and the castle of Hikone on its shore, one of the
few in Japan that still keeps its original tower.

35
00:05:43.907 --> 00:05:46.589 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Shizuoka into view.

36
00:05:49.892 --> 00:06:00.086 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Shizuoka faces the Pacific on the south coast of central Honshū, between
Kanagawa and Aichi, with the city of Hamamatsu in the west and Mount Fuji in
the north.

37
00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:14.136 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It grows about 40% of Japan’s tea on the slopes below Fuji, and Fuji’s
southern half stands here, at 3,776 metres.

38
00:06:14.796 --> 00:06:21.483 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the quiet hill of tea under Fuji, and the plastic models and musical
instruments made in the same prefecture.

39
00:06:22.154 --> 00:06:23.988 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Aichi into view.

40
00:06:28.809 --> 00:06:42.799 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Aichi faces the Pacific in the middle of Honshū, between Shizuoka and Mie,
with the city of Nagoya, the country’s fourth largest, on the bay and Gifu and
Nagano behind it.

41
00:06:43.455 --> 00:06:52.406 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Toyota is based here and takes its name from the founder’s family, with the
spelling changed from Toyoda because eight brush strokes are thought luckier.

42
00:06:53.070 --> 00:07:02.242 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the bay where all three warlords who unified Japan were born, and the
carmaker that changed its name for luck.

43
00:07:02.924 --> 00:07:05.478 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Hyōgo into view.

44
00:07:09.425 --> 00:07:21.151 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Hyōgo runs along the Sea of Japan coast of western Honshū and round to the
Inland Sea, with the city of Kōbe on Osaka Bay and Tottori at its northern
end.

45
00:07:21.815 --> 00:07:38.742 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The earthquake of 1995 killed more than 6,400 people here and changed Japanese
building codes, while Himeji castle to the west came through it undamaged.

46
00:07:39.405 --> 00:07:47.207 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the arsenal of the seventh century, Kobe beef, and the white castle
that shrugged the earthquake off.

47
00:07:47.884 --> 00:07:49.753 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ōsaka into view.

48
00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:05.183 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ōsaka sits at the mouth of the Yodo river on Osaka Bay in western Honshū, with
the city of Ōsaka in the middle and Wakayama and Nara to the south.

49
00:08:05.834 --> 00:08:19.778 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The world’s first futures exchange traded rice here from 1697, and the people
still greet each other by asking whether business is good.

50
00:08:20.432 --> 00:08:29.859 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the merchant capital and the kitchen of the country, where a good
meal is said to eat you bankrupt.

51
00:08:30.552 --> 00:08:32.421 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Mie into view.

52
00:08:37.442 --> 00:08:45.197 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mie wraps the Kii Peninsula on the Pacific in the middle of Honshū, between
Aichi and Wakayama, with the city of Tsu on the Ise bay.

53
00:08:45.863 --> 00:08:55.836 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ise Jingū here is the most sacred Shinto shrine and has been rebuilt from new
timber every twenty years for thirteen centuries.

54
00:08:56.498 --> 00:09:03.743 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the three-fold prince, the rebuilding of the shrine, and the pearls
invented in Ago Bay.

55
00:09:04.449 --> 00:09:06.446 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Nara into view.

56
00:09:13.626 --> 00:09:23.599 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nara is inland south of Kyōto, between Ōsaka and Mie with Wakayama below it,
and the old city of Nara in its northern basin.

57
00:09:24.263 --> 00:09:35.652 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was Japan’s first permanent capital, from 710 to 784, and its Tōdai-ji
temple holds the largest bronze Buddha in the world.

58
00:09:36.311 --> 00:09:43.173 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the levelled basin, the great bronze Buddha, and the twelve hundred
deer that walk in the park.

59
00:09:43.866 --> 00:09:47.012 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Wakayama into view.

60
00:09:53.330 --> 00:10:03.140 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Wakayama takes the southern tip of the Kii Peninsula on the Pacific, with Nara
to the north, Mie to the east and the city of Wakayama at the narrow neck.

61
00:10:03.792 --> 00:10:11.977 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Kumano Kodō pilgrimage routes cross it, one of only two pilgrimage roads
on the UNESCO list, the other being the road to Santiago de Compostela.

62
00:10:12.638 --> 00:10:20.870 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the young mountain, the plum vinegar, and the mandarin orchards on
the warm southern slopes.

63
00:10:26.768 --> 00:10:49.299 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
15 regions of Japan, one for each target of the map: Saitama, Gifu, Tokyo,
Yamanashi, Chiba, Kanagawa, Kyōto, Shiga, Shizuoka, Aichi, Hyōgo, Ōsaka, Mie,
Nara and Wakayama.

64
00:10:49.952 --> 00:10:59.507 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of Japan from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
